@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1723 = private unnamed_addr constant [925 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for assignments to `os.environ`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, `os.environ` is a mapping that represents the environment of the\0Acurrent process.\0A\0AHowever, reassigning to `os.environ` does not clear the environment. Instead,\0Ait merely updates the `os.environ` for the current process. This can lead to\0Aunexpected behavior, especially when running the program in a subprocess.\0A\0AInstead, use `os.environ.clear()` to clear the environment, or use the\0A`env` argument of `subprocess.Popen` to pass a custom environment to\0Aa subprocess.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport os\0A\0Aos.environ = {\22foo\22: \22bar\22}\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport os\0A\0Aos.environ.clear()\0Aos.environ[\22foo\22] = \22bar\22\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `os.environ`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.environ)\0A- [Python documentation: `subprocess.Popen`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#subprocess.Popen)\0A", align 1
