@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.2030 = private unnamed_addr constant [1492 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the `exit()` and `quit()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`exit` and `quit` come from the `site` module, which is typically imported\0Aautomatically during startup. However, it is not _guaranteed_ to be\0Aimported, and so using these functions may result in a `NameError` at\0Aruntime. Generally, these constants are intended to be used in an interactive\0Ainterpreter, and not in programs.\0A\0APrefer `sys.exit()`, as the `sys` module is guaranteed to exist in all\0Acontexts.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is always unsafe. When replacing `exit` or `quit` with `sys.exit`,\0Athe behavior can change in the following ways:\0A\0A1. If the code runs in an environment where the `site` module is not imported\0A   (e.g., with `python -S`), the original code would raise a `NameError`, while\0A   the fixed code would execute normally.\0A\0A2. `site.exit` and `sys.exit` handle tuple arguments differently. `site.exit`\0A   treats tuples as regular objects and always returns exit code 1, while `sys.exit`\0A   interprets tuple contents to determine the exit code: an empty tuple () results in\0A   exit code 0, and a single-element tuple like (2,) uses that element's value (2) as\0A   the exit code.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aif __name__ == \22__main__\22:\0A    exit()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif __name__ == \22__main__\22:\0A    sys.exit()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Constants added by the `site` module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/constants.html#constants-added-by-the-site-module)\0A", align 1
