@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.2076 = private unnamed_addr constant [862 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `from_float` and `from_decimal` usages to construct\0A`Decimal` and `Fraction` instances.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASince Python 3.2, the `Fraction` and `Decimal` classes can be constructed\0Aby passing float or decimal instances to the constructor directly. As such,\0Athe use of `from_float` and `from_decimal` methods is unnecessary, and\0Ashould be avoided in favor of the more concise constructor syntax.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0ADecimal.from_float(4.2)\0ADecimal.from_float(float(\22inf\22))\0AFraction.from_float(4.2)\0AFraction.from_decimal(Decimal(\224.2\22))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0ADecimal(4.2)\0ADecimal(\22inf\22)\0AFraction(4.2)\0AFraction(Decimal(4.2))\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `decimal`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/decimal.html)\0A- [Python documentation: `fractions`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/fractions.html)\0A", align 1
